<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shigatse</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shigatse is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> region west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. It grew around the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse-dzong">Samdrubtse</a>, seat of the Rinpungpa and Tsangpa rulers of Tsang in the 15th to 17th centuries. Its Tashilhunpo Monastery, founded in 1447, is the traditional seat of the Panchen Lamas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shigatse.jpg" alt="Shigatse" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Taveneaux · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shigatse_Dzong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was divided into eight chikyaps, each subdivided into dzongs and estates: Lhasa, administering 29 dzongs, Changgyu with its seat at Nagqu, Lhoka at Tsetang, Dakong, Ngari at Gar, Chomo, <b>Shigatse</b>, and Domed or Kham with its seat at Chamdo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1435, after a succession crisis at the Phagmodru court, Norbu Zangpo of Rinpung seized the fortress of Samdrubtse (<b>Shigatse</b>), and the family became the real power in Tsang while still nominally serving the Phagmodru kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/rinpungpa">Rinpungpa</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhingshak Tseten Dorje, an official of the Rinpungpa at Samdrubtse (<b>Shigatse</b>), rebelled and by 1565 had replaced his masters as ruler of Tsang.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsangpa-dynasty">Tsangpa dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
